Ludwerk: a lost D3D12 device crashes the first draw that needs a descriptor heap.

0002 and 0004 stopped SDL reading through the NULL uniform buffer a lost
device hands back. A descriptor heap is the same NULL by another door. A
command buffer takes its two GPU descriptor heaps from a pool at its first
draw or dispatch, and again whenever a draw's tables might not fit
(D3D12_INTERNAL_SetGPUDescriptorHeaps). The pool is refilled as earlier
command buffers finish; when it is empty a heap is created, and on a lost
device that fails -- "Failed to create descriptor heap!" -- and returns NULL.
SetGPUDescriptorHeaps then reads `viewHeap->handle`, and the process dies of
an access violation inside SDL.

Whether the pool is empty at that moment is a race with the frames still in
flight, so the same removal ended one run cleanly and killed the next: seen on
a hosted Windows runner whose software device was removed under a test,
after "Failed to map buffer pool!" three times and "Could
not create buffer!" forty.

The fix leaves the command buffer with no heaps when either could not be had,
and a draw or dispatch with no heaps is not recorded: the bind functions say
so and their callers return. The draw is going nowhere -- there is no device
to draw it -- and the error stays set, so the submit that follows fails and
the application is told its device is gone. Still present upstream on
2026-10-12.

